{"name":"Wistman’s Wood","hex":"#aa9966","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance making up the central part of the trunk and branches of a tree. Used as a material for construction, to manufacture various items, etc. or as fuel.","The wood from a particular species of tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or plant with trees.","To hide behind trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wistman's Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wistman's Wood is one of Britain's most famous ancient temperate rainforests and one of only a few remote remaining high-altitude oakwoods on Dartmoor in Devon, England. The first written document to mention Wistman's Wood dates to the 17th century, while more recent tree-ring studies show that individual trees could be many hundreds of years old."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Wistman%27s_Wood_-_one_of_Britain%27s_last_remaining_ancient_temperate_rainforests.jpg/330px-Wistman%27s_Wood_-_one_of_Britain%27s_last_remaining_ancient_temperate_rainforests.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wistman's_Wood","wikidata":"Q3569474"},{"title":"Wood","blocks":[{"text":"Wood is a natural, lignocellulosic material derived primarily from the secondary xylem of trees and other woody plants, where it provides mechanical support and in the functional sapwood, conducts water and dissolved minerals. Being a natural material, it is characterized as an organic material – a natural composite of cellulosic fibers that are strong in tension and embedded in a matrix of lignin and hemicelluloses that resists compression."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/16_wood_samples.jpg/330px-16_wood_samples.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood","wikidata":"Q287"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Wistman%27s_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg/500px-Wistman%27s_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Wistman%27s_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg/1280px-Wistman%27s_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Wistman%27s_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg/500px-Wistman%27s_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"View over the wood","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWistman's_Wood%2C_Dartmoor_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4801741.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/500px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/1280px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/TreeKnot.jpg/500px-TreeKnot.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A knot on a tree trunk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATreeKnot.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wood_Knot.JPG/500px-Wood_Knot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wood_Knot.JPG/1280px-Wood_Knot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Wood_Knot.JPG/500px-Wood_Knot.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wood knot in vertical section","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWood_Knot.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Taxus_wood.jpg/330px-Taxus_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Taxus_wood.jpg/330px-Taxus_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A section of a yew branch showing 27 annual growth rings, pale sapwood, dark heartwood, and pith (center dark spot). The dark radial lines are small knots.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATaxus_wood.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Cross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg/500px-Cross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Cross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg/1280px-Cross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Cross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg/500px-Cross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cross-section of an oak log showing growth rings","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACross-section_of_an_Oak_Log_Showing_Growth_Rings.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sequoia_wood.jpg/500px-Sequoia_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Sequoia_wood.jpg/500px-Sequoia_wood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th